<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Hermunduri</h1><p class="st-meta">1 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Germanic people of Thuringia and the upper Elbe, unusually friendly to Rome, who were allowed to trade freely inside the province of Raetia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Hermunduri were an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe-germanic-peoples">Elbe Germanic</a> people who, around 1 BCE, were resettled by the Roman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-domitius-ahenobarbus-consul-16-bce">Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus</a> in lands vacated by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marcomanni">Marcomanni</a> along the upper Main. Their territory came to stretch from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a> north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> calls them faithful to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and notes that, alone among Germans, they could enter the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augusta Vindelicorum</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Augsburg</a>) without guard to trade. In 19 their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vibilius">Vibilius</a> helped overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catualda">Catualda</a>, and in 50 he also brought down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a>. In 58 they defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chatti">Chatti</a> in a war over sacred salt springs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Hermunduri took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 160s and 170s. They disappear from sources in the 3rd century, and most historians see them as one of the roots of the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-thuringians">Thuringians</a>, whose name may derive from theirs.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hermunduri.jpg" alt="Hermunduri" width="320" height="159"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suebi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vibilius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elbe Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=hermunduri" aria-label="Hermunduri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4885 417 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hermunduri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5205 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5205 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=hermunduri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
